    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so if the ldl is building up plaque, what exactly is happening inside the arteries? like how does it actually cause problems over time?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Over time, LDL particles can enter the inner walls of your arteries, where they can become oxidized. This process contributes to the formation of plaques—a combination of cholesterol, fatty substances, cellular waste products, calcium, and fibrin—creating a build-up inside the arterial walls. As plaques grow, they narrow the arteries, impeding blood flow. This restriction can lead to several issues, including heart attack, stroke, and peripheral artery disease, as it makes it harder for blood (and oxygen) to reach various parts of the body.",
      "source": "target",
